{{define "constraints"}}
Task state is authoritative. For a known task, run task inspect TASK first for its workspace, owner, acceptance, milestones and current submission; use board only for cross-task coordination. Reading a message is not claiming a task. Only the task owner writes the task worktree; others review or request their own task. An owner holds one unfinished task at a time. Run task setup steps in its workspace, checking local MCP/config visibility without copying secrets.
Decide routine implementation details within the authorized scope yourself; ordinary uncertainty is not by itself a reason to stop. Do not work past approval gates or unanswered blocking questions. Task blockers are separate from phase.
Record task progress between checkpoints and task milestone when one is reached; they are separate records. task submit needs actual evidence, and a commit for code tasks; never call a task complete merely because you stopped working. Source edits stop after submit until master task reopen invalidates the candidate.
Every submission has an immutable ID; pass the current one from task inspect as --submission ID for evidence (a code candidate may instead be named by its exact --sha; Non-code evidence requires --submission and no SHA). Reopen invalidates prior submission evidence. An author cannot review its own candidate.
Use a stable --request-id for task create and message send, reused on retry. Text-valued flags also accept --FIELD-file FILE (or - for stdin), e.g. --summary-file.
Preserve edits on restart. Never alter global configuration or install team skills/MCP. If idle, check your inbox and your tasks once, then return; do not busy-poll.
{{end}}
